[
    {
        "content": "<p>Hi all, I am Sela, a cryptography researcher that is slowly learning Lean to keep up with the times. Am new here, so let me know if this is not the right place for this.</p>\n<p>One of the results from OpenAI today is <a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math/blob/main/preprints/Matrix-Multiplication-Nine-Fourths-October-2-2026/paper.pdf\">a new matrix multiplication upper bound</a> showing that <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>2.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega \\leq 2.25</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7719em;vertical-align:-0.136em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">ω</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≤</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2.25</span></span></span></span> over the complex numbers. I was surprised that the underlying field mattered, so tried to understand why it is restricted to <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{C}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">C</span></span></span></span>.</p>\n<p>Pretty quickly Codex generalized it to any field and has a <a href=\"https://github.com/selanavot/matrix-multiplication-all-fields\">Lean proof from a fork of their repo</a>. That is, <strong>the resulting Lean theorem states that over any field <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathbb{F}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">F</span></span></span></span>, <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ω</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">(</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">)</mo><mo>≤</mo><mn>2.25</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\omega(\\mathbb{F}) \\leq 2.25</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:1em;vertical-align:-0.25em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0359em;\">ω</span><span class=\"mopen\">(</span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">F</span><span class=\"mclose\">)</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≤</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2.25</span></span></span></span>.</strong></p>\n<p>Would appreciate thoughts on what to do with it from here, and whether (or how) to make it useful.</p>",
        "id": 629733793,
        "sender_full_name": "Sela Navot",
        "timestamp": 1791348756
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Cool! If I'm reading <a href=\"https://github.com/selanavot/matrix-multiplication-all-fields/blob/76b2937ebeb56ace5f2f4a895238d195722c5069/lean/OAI/LinearAlgebra/MatrixMultiplication/Model.lean\">this</a> correctly, it looks like the result is about the existence of a family of algebraic circuits. Specifically, the quantifiers are:</p>\n<p><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2.25</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>τ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>n</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">∀\\, a &gt; 2.25, ∃\\, τ &lt; a, ∀\\, ε &gt; 0, ∃\\, C &gt; 0, ∀\\, n,</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7335em;vertical-align:-0.0391em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∀</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">&gt;</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2.25</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∃</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1132em;\">τ</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">&lt;</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∀</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">ε</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">&gt;</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∃</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">&gt;</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∀</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span></span></span> there exists an algebraic circuit (DAG of add/sub/mul/input/constant) that correctly calculates <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n × n × n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span></span></span></span> matrix multiplication, with at most <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C n^{τ+ε}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7713em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.7713em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.1132em;\">τ</span><span class=\"mbin mtight\">+</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">ε</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> add/sub/mul gates.<br>\n(wires don't count, so you can copy a value as much as you want)</p>\n<p>Do you know if the result also shows the existence of a uniform algorithm, not just a family of circuits?<br>\nAnd is it possible to formalize, perhaps using CSLib's Turing machines?</p>\n<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"1211964\">Sela Navot</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/matrix.20multiplication.20algorithms/near/629733793\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Would appreciate thoughts on what to do with it from here, and whether (or how) to make it useful.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You can try running <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/comparator\">#comparator</a> with that file as a challenge (plus the <code>≤ 2.25</code> theorem, and the <code>BddBelow</code> theorem), to make sure that the proof is correct.</p>\n<p>Can you generalize beyond fields, e.g. to <code>CommRing</code>s?<br>\nCan you remove subtraction gates? Aren't they redundant since you can do <code>a + (-1) * b</code> with only double the cost?</p>",
        "id": 629741545,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1791352636
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"933054\">Snir Broshi</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/matrix.20multiplication.20algorithms/near/629741545\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>And is it possible to formalize, perhaps using CSLib's Turing machines?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Or even with the more-recently-defined <a href=\"https://api.cslib.io/docs/Cslib/Computability/Circuit/Basic.html\">circuits</a> (which allow arbitrary signature and interpretation)</p>",
        "id": 629764196,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Waring",
        "timestamp": 1791359740
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thank you! This is very helpful.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Do you know if the result also shows the existence of a uniform algorithm, not just a family of circuits?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The proof chooses one finite multiplication scheme and applies it recursively, so it seems to give a uniform arithmetic algorithm. Will spend time on it today to try to understand and formalize that.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>And is it possible to formalize, perhaps using CSLib's Turing machines?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Maybe? This is way beyond my knowledge in Lean, but we would need some finite encoding of field elements and algorithms for arithmetic operations on them, or work over some generic field model. The circuit abstraction seems promising, or some other arithmetic machine. Will look -- am sure to learn something.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>You can try running <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover/comparator\">#comparator</a> with that file as a challenge (plus the <code>≤ 2.25</code> theorem, and the <code>BddBelow</code> theorem), to make sure that the proof is correct.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><a href=\"https://github.com/selanavot/matrix-multiplication-all-fields/blob/verify/comparator/verification/comparator/README.md\">Done</a>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Can you remove subtraction gates? Aren't they redundant since you can do <code>a + (-1) * b</code> with only double the cost?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes probably, though wanted to keep the same circuit definition as OpenAI to make the comparison easier</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Can you generalize beyond fields, e.g. to <code>CommRing</code>s?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is interesting. Am still parsing through the proofs but according to Codex is uses inverses of nonzero elements and a descent argument based on finite-dimensional vector spaces, so it does not immediately translates into rings. Would be a fun direction to look into.</p>",
        "id": 629835195,
        "sender_full_name": "Sela Navot",
        "timestamp": 1791376697
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"933054\">Snir Broshi</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/matrix.20multiplication.20algorithms/near/629741545\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2.25</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>τ</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>ε</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>n</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">∀\\, a &gt; 2.25, ∃\\, τ &lt; a, ∀\\, ε &gt; 0, ∃\\, C &gt; 0, ∀\\, n,</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7335em;vertical-align:-0.0391em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∀</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">&gt;</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2.25</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∃</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.1132em;\">τ</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">&lt;</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∀</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">ε</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">&gt;</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∃</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">&gt;</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∀</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span></span></span> there exists an algebraic circuit (DAG of add/sub/mul/input/constant) that correctly calculates <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n × n × n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span></span></span></span> matrix multiplication, with at most <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>τ</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>ε</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C n^{τ+ε}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7713em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.7713em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\" style=\"margin-right:0.1132em;\">τ</span><span class=\"mbin mtight\">+</span><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">ε</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> add/sub/mul gates.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Isn't this equivalent to:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>a</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2.25</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∃</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>C</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">∀</mi><mtext> </mtext><mi>n</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">∀\\, a &gt; 2.25, ∃\\, C &gt; 0, ∀\\, n,</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7335em;vertical-align:-0.0391em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∀</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">a</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">&gt;</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2.25</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∃</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">&gt;</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8889em;vertical-align:-0.1944em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">0</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">∀</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span><span class=\"mpunct\">,</span></span></span></span> there exists an algebraic circuit (DAG of add/sub/mul/input/constant) that correctly calculates <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>×</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n × n × n</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6667em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.4306em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span></span></span></span> matrix multiplication, with at most <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>a</mi></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C n^a</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\" style=\"margin-right:0.0715em;\">C</span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.6644em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal mtight\">a</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> add/sub/mul gates.</p>\n</blockquote>",
