    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This 2.25 result also seems to hold for every semiring with a field inside it (matrices over <code>R</code> such that <code>[Semiring R] [Field K] [Algebra K R]</code>).<br>\nThis includes simple rings, division rings, rings of prime characteristic, and polynomials and matrices of any of these.<br>\nSo we can multiply matrices of polynomials of matrices of polynomials of rationals with a good complexity!<br>\n(of operations in the ring, not of operations on rationals <span aria-label=\"sweat smile\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f605\" role=\"img\" title=\"sweat smile\">:sweat_smile:</span>)</p>\n<p>It makes me wonder what's the exponent for <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ℤ</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord amsrm\">Z</span></span></span></span>, where even though in practice we would just multiply over <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Q</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ℚ</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8556em;vertical-align:-0.1667em;\"></span><span class=\"mord amsrm\">Q</span></span></span></span>, we technically can't divide in circuits for <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ℤ</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord amsrm\">Z</span></span></span></span> so this bound doesn't hold.</p>\n<p>btw including the input &amp; constant gates in the circuit size doesn't hurt the complexity (and also removing subtraction gates).</p>",
        "id": 630097244,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1791453987
    },
    {
        "content": "<blockquote>\n<p>This 2.25 result also seems to hold for every semiring with a field inside it</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is very cool! I will say, from an aesthetic perspective, it is much nicer if the bound holds for matrices ofd matrices.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>It makes me wonder what's the exponent for <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ℤ</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord amsrm\">Z</span></span></span></span></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Maybe I am missing something, but can't we convert each integer <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n \\in \\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.5782em;vertical-align:-0.0391em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">∈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">Z</span></span></span></span> to $$\\frac{n}{1} \\in \\mathbb{Q}$, then do the multiplication there, and convert back at the end? <del>Or more abstractly, every arithmetic circuit in $\\mathbb{Q}$ can be seen as an arithmetic circuit over the integers with the inputs being the numerators and denominators.</del> </p>\n<p><strong>Edit:</strong> <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"933054\">@Snir Broshi</span> pointed out that this is wrong, adding an integer division circuit at the end adds a dependency on the size of the integer</p>",
        "id": 630136706,
        "sender_full_name": "Sela Navot",
        "timestamp": 1791464137
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"1211964\">Sela Navot</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/matrix.20multiplication.20algorithms/near/630136706\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Maybe I am missing something, but can't we convert each integer <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n \\in \\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.5782em;vertical-align:-0.0391em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">∈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord mathbb\">Z</span></span></span></span> to $$\\frac{n}{1} \\in \\mathbb{Q}$, then do the multiplication there, and convert back at the end?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes, that's the \"in practice\" part I mentioned</p>\n<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"1211964\">Sela Navot</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/matrix.20multiplication.20algorithms/near/630136706\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Or more abstractly, every arithmetic circuit in $\\mathbb{Q}$ can be seen as an arithmetic circuit over the integers with the inputs being the numerators and denominators.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>No, unless you change the problem to allow dividing the numerators by their denominators at the end</p>",
        "id": 630181306,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1791472775
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"933054\">Snir Broshi</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/matrix.20multiplication.20algorithms/near/630097244\">said</a> in <a class=\"stream-topic\" data-stream-id=\"113489\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/matrix.20multiplication.20algorithms/with/630181306\">#new members &gt; matrix multiplication algorithms</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>It makes me wonder what's the exponent for <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">ℤ</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6889em;\"></span><span class=\"mord amsrm\">Z</span></span></span></span></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>After working for the majority of a night, Codex managed to extend the proof of the 9/4 bound to any associative unital ring (not necessarily commutative).</p>\n<p>Am parsing through it, but the Lean verification passes and I am pretty sure the formalization is sound.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://github.com/selanavot/omega_le_nine_quarters_all_rings\">https://github.com/selanavot/omega_le_nine_quarters_all_rings</a></p>",
        "id": 630500934,
        "sender_full_name": "Sela Navot",
        "timestamp": 1791570266
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Wow! If you remove subtraction gates, could you extend to <code>Semiring</code>s?</p>",
        "id": 630502228,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1791570599
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"933054\">Snir Broshi</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113489-new-members/topic/matrix.20multiplication.20algorithms/near/630502228\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Wow! If you remove subtraction gates, could you extend to <code>Semiring</code>s?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I think not. According to Codex there is no natural way to extend the algorithm, and there is a lower bound of <span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">n^3</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\"><span class=\"mord mathnormal\">n</span><span class=\"msupsub\"><span class=\"vlist-t\"><span class=\"vlist-r\"><span class=\"vlist\" style=\"height:0.8141em;\"><span style=\"top:-3.063em;margin-right:0.05em;\"><span class=\"pstrut\" style=\"height:2.7em;\"></span><span class=\"sizing reset-size6 size3 mtight\"><span class=\"mord mtight\">3</span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span></span> for semirings  in <a href=\"https://dl.acm.org/doi/pdf/10.1145/322326.322341\">§4.1 of this 1982 paper</a> by Jerrum and Snir.</p>",
        "id": 630517177,
        "sender_full_name": "Sela Navot",
        "timestamp": 1791574388
    }
]